.TH WORDSWIFT "1" "October 2026" "WordSwift 0.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
wordswift \- semantic word search engine with a MeiliSearch-compatible API
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wordswift
.I COMMAND
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
WordSwift is a word embedding engine derived from fastText that also provides
full-text BM25 search, typo-tolerant fuzzy search, prefix completion and
semantic similarity through a single MeiliSearch-compatible REST API, backed by
LevelDB for persistence.
.PP
The command line tool trains and queries embedding models and runs the API
server. When installed from the Debian package the server is managed by systemd;
see
.BR wordswift.service (5).
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B train \fIcorpus\fR \fB\-o\fR \fImodel\fR [\fB\-\-dim\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-ws\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-epoch\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-min\-count\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-lr\fR \fIF\fR]
Train word embeddings from a text corpus and write the model to \fImodel\fR.
Defaults: dim 100, window size 5, 5 epochs, min-count 5, learning rate 0.05.
.TP
.B nearest \fImodel\fR \fIword\fR [\fB\-n\fR \fIN\fR]
Print the \fIN\fR words nearest to \fIword\fR by cosine similarity.
.TP
.B analogies \fImodel\fR \fIa\fR \fIb\fR \fIc\fR [\fB\-n\fR \fIN\fR]
Solve \fIa\fR is to \fIb\fR as \fIc\fR is to what, and print \fIN\fR candidates.
.TP
.B vector \fImodel\fR \fIword\fR [\fB\-\-json\fR]
Print the embedding vector for \fIword\fR. With \fB\-\-json\fR emit a JSON object.
.TP
.B serve [\fB\-\-host\fR \fIaddr\fR] [\fB\-\-port\fR \fIn\fR] [\fB\-\-data\-dir\fR \fIdir\fR]
Start the HTTP API server. Defaults to \fI0.0.0.0\fR, port \fI7700\fR and data
directory \fIdata\fR (relative to the working directory unless overridden).
.TP
.B create\-index \fIname\fR [\fB\-\-dim\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-data\-dir\fR \fIdir\fR]
Create a new index without starting the server.
.TP
.B list\-indexes [\fB\-\-data\-dir\fR \fIdir\fR]
List the indexes present in the data directory.
.TP
.B import \fIinput\fR \fB\-o\fR \fImodel\fR
Import a word2vec text-format vector file into a WordSwift model.
.TP
.B index \fImodel\fR [\fB\-\-db\-path\fR \fIdir\fR]
Load a trained model into LevelDB for fast lookups.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B WORDSWIFT_DATA_DIR
Directory holding indexes and \fImanifest.json\fR. Used as the default when
\fBserve\fR is given no \fB\-\-data\-dir\fR. The flag takes precedence and is
exported so worker processes agree on the location.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/lib/wordswift
Index storage: \fImanifest.json\fR plus a \fI<name>_index\fR and \fI<name>_ft\fR
LevelDB directory per index. Created and owned by the \fBwordswift\fR system
user; provisioned by systemd \fBStateDirectory=\fR and \fBtmpfiles.d\fR rather
than by the package, so the service user can write to it.
.TP
.I /etc/wordswift/wordswift.env
Optional environment overrides read by the systemd unit.
.B WORDSWIFT_HOST
and
.B WORDSWIFT_PORT
are substituted into the unit's
.B ExecStart
command line; only
.B WORDSWIFT_DATA_DIR
is read by the application itself. The file uses systemd EnvironmentFile syntax
(no \fBexport\fR, no quoting, no command substitution).
.TP
.I /usr/lib/wordswift
Private installation directory for the Python sources.
.SH SECURITY
The server has
.B no built-in authentication
and enables CORS for all origins. The packaged unit therefore binds to
\fB127.0.0.1\fR by default. Do not expose port 7700 to untrusted networks; put a
reverse proxy that enforces authentication in front of it, or bind to a private
network only. See \fI/usr/share/doc/wordswift\fR and the upstream
\fIdocs/DOCKER.md\fR for an nginx example.
.SH SYSTEMD
The package installs \fBwordswift.service\fR, enabled but not started until
configured. Typical use:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
systemctl status wordswift
journalctl -u wordswift -f
.fi
.RE
.PP
The unit runs as the unprivileged \fBwordswift\fR user with sandboxing enabled
(\fBProtectSystem\fR, \fBPrivateTmp\fR, \fBNoNewPrivileges\fR, \fBSystemCallFilter\fR
and others), and stops with \fBKillMode=process\fR so the server can close its
LevelDB handles. A \fBSIGKILL\fR would leave a stale \fBLOCK\fR file behind and
prevent the next start from opening the database.
.PP
Because indexes are LevelDB directories holding an
.B exclusive
lock, only one server process may use a given data directory. Do not run the
service and a foreground \fBwordswift serve\fR against the same directory.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero on success. Non-zero on error, such as an unparseable corpus, a missing
model file, or an unbindable port.
.SH BUGS
Upstream issue tracker: <https://github.com/djinn/WordSwift/issues>.
.PP
Known limitation: the HTTP API can delete a whole index
(\fBDELETE /indexes/{name}\fR) but has no per-document delete endpoint.
